im when would people like  sterling who need rvs or mobile homes to  live in right now get them i would say  that we'll probably start placing  trailers sometime  in in the november time frame  that's another month  yes sir that's not the only thing that  we've got going the non-congregate  sheltering our transitional sheltering  assistants where people can go get a  hotel room today i tried to get a motel  room close by but there ain't you know  sterling told us the reason he didn't  get approved to get money from fema to  rebuild his home is because he couldn't  prove that he owned the property fema  used to require you to give him a deed  but they changed that policy just  recently you can now give them let's say  a letter from a local mayor that says  you own it or you can show them a home  improvement receipt or proof that you've  paid property taxes so sterling went  digging looking for proof even went down  to the tax office  they're closed closed until further  notice and the number was disconnected  we called fema back and that's when they  did something very rare  fema accepted this handwritten signed  letter from guidry he wrote i have lived  on the property for 31 years saying his  mother passed away but before she died  she told me that the property is mine  in the meantime i remember that they  were they hauled off my tiny house in  big sections so i went to the trash pile  this morning  and cut out part of the floor  that was still together to make my  foundation this is where sterling and  bree are going to bathe  but until it's ready  that should be enough for you my baby  they are bathing with water bottles at a  porta potty five minutes down the road  it's been hard depressing sad  it's hard on my dad and he just like  holds it in so  you know a person can only take so much  on this night look look their tent was  infested with bugs  and so they went back to the truck  for night number 29.  you can see the bugs right now  look fema has fast tracked this man's  story as early as today  we expect that fema will approve  sterling and give him up to 36 000  so he can rebuild his home  he told me david i have 200 to my name  that's it every little bit helps but i  want you to know that there are  thousands of people  like sterling in southeast louisiana  right now  every little bit helps  including picking up the trash it's been  a month since the storm gail a month  and the trash is still at the road  and david that's why it's so important  that you tell these stories because  they're facts and figures but to these  people these are real life people living  this life his daughter's beautiful by  the way i think she summed it up nicely  it's hard depressing and sad it's  heartbreaking to see he was denied when  you see how he's living thank you david  thank you very much
 

